{
  "note": "Shared truth for drawing a rich-text message: the RichDoc v2 document the core stores (`docJson`, flare-proto `RichTextContent.doc_json`, schema flare-proto/schemas/rich_doc_v2.schema.json) read into the blocks and runs a body draws. The core validates every document it accepts and every one it receives, and degrades an invalid one to a text message; a kit still reads defensively, because a body never trusts a document more than the core does. `doc` is the document: a JSON object, or a string holding one (the wire form). `generate` stands in for a document too large to write out: `{\"paragraphTextNodes\": n}` is one paragraph holding n text nodes each reading `a`; `{\"nestedQuotes\": n}` is n quotes each holding the next, the innermost holding one paragraph with one text node reading `a`. `blocks` is what is drawn, or null when the document is not drawable and the body shows the message's `plainText` instead; a generated case states only `drawable`. Drawable: the root is an object whose `type` is `doc`, whose `version` is 2 and whose `children` is an array; no node sits deeper than 64 (the root is depth 0, its blocks 1, their inlines 2…); there are at most 10000 nodes counting the root, blocks and inlines (marks are not nodes). These are the core's own limits (`validate.rs`). Anything else, including a string that is not JSON, is not drawable. Blocks: `paragraph` and `heading` draw their inline children as runs and are dropped when they have none; a heading's `level` is clamped to 1…6 (a missing or non-numeric level reads as 1). `quote` draws its block children and is dropped when none remain. `code_block` draws the text of its children (a `text` node's text, `\\n` for a `hard_break`) with its `language` when that is a non-empty string, and is dropped when the text is empty. `bullet_list` and `ordered_list` draw each `list_item` child as an item of blocks; other children are skipped, an item with no blocks is dropped, and a list with no items is dropped. `divider` draws a rule. `custom_block` draws its block children in its place (a Markdown table arrives as one with none, and draws nothing). A block of any other type, a `list_item` outside a list included, is skipped. Runs, in document order and never merged: `text` is a run with its text and its marks — the known ones (`bold`, `italic`, `underline`, `strike`, `spoiler`) once each in that order, unknown ones ignored — and is dropped when its text is empty. `inline_code` is a run marked `code`, dropped when its text is empty. `hard_break` is a run reading `\\n`. `mention` reads its `text`, or `@` and its `user_id` when it has no text, and carries `mention` (the user id, possibly empty); one with neither is dropped. `emoji` reads its `text`, or `:key:` when it has no text, and carries `emoji` (the key, possibly empty); one with neither is dropped. `link` draws its children's runs, each carrying `link` = the link's `href` exactly as written when `safeExternalUrl` accepts it; a link it refuses draws the same runs without a `link` of its own, so a run inside nested links carries the innermost link that was accepted. `custom_inline` draws nothing; an inline of any other type draws its `text` as a plain run when it has one. An outline omits what a run or block does not have: no `marks` when there are none, no `language` without one. `spoilerCovers`: what a covered spoiler run draws in place of its words, so the words are in neither the rendered text nor a copy until the reader reveals them: every Unicode code point that is not whitespace becomes U+3000 IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE (one em wide, a line may break after it), and whitespace stays. The run's ground is drawn in the text colour, and assistive technology hears the kit's spoiler name instead. The four kits read this file in their tests.",
